{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And HaShem regretted that He had made man on the earth.</b> With the joining of the earth, as I explained in the name of the Ramban at the verse 'Let us make man' — I and the earth, for the earth gave within him coarse and thick matter. And the matter of this regret and the matter of 'and it grieved Him to His heart' is a deep, deep derashah — who can find it? All who search it out have lengthened over it to their weariness, this one saying thus and that one saying thus, and all of them as it were prophesying. And so too many matters in the Torah are sealed and closed, regarding all of which my heart says and concludes not to lengthen in these derashot, for the human intellect would grow weary of grasping them, for all those doubts come to us from the side that we estimate His knowledge, blessed be He, according to our knowledge, whereas the verse states the reverse of this: ״כִּי לֹא מַחְשְׁבוֹתַי מַחְשְׁבוֹתֵיכֶם״ etc. (Isaiah 55:8) — so how can we stand upon His thought, blessed be He, which is unknown among us, and we have no power to estimate it?"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The phrase הֶאֱרִיכוּ בּוֹ לְמַעֲנִיתָם is textually difficult; read hyper-literally as 'they lengthened over it to their weariness,' though the reading may reflect a corruption. The closing of the Isaiah citation after וְגוֹ׳ follows the Hebrew as given."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:21.056983170Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}